    "content": "The Somali government and the Libyan government have agreed to strengthen coordination and collaboration, particularly on matters concerning travel and the situation of Somali citizens in Libya. The agreement was reached during a meeting held in New York on the sidelines of the 83rd session of the United Nations General Assembly.\n\nSomali Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation Abshir Omar Jama held talks with Dahir Saleh Al-Baour, who manages the affairs of the Libyan Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation. The two officials discussed ways to enhance bilateral relations and address the challenges facing Somali nationals in Libya.\n\nThis development comes amid ongoing efforts by Somalia to improve the treatment and protection of its citizens abroad, especially in countries with significant Somali migrant populations. Libya has long been a transit point for migrants and refugees seeking to reach Europe, and many Somalis have faced difficult conditions there.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this agreement signals a potential improvement in the handling of travel documents, consular services, and the overall welfare of Somalis in Libya. It also reflects broader diplomatic engagement by Somalia to secure better protections for its citizens and to foster cooperation with North African states.\n\nThe meeting in New York underscores the importance of international platforms like the UN General Assembly for addressing bilateral issues and advancing the interests of Somali citizens worldwide. Further details on the implementation of the agreement are expected in the coming months.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/25/soomaaliya-iyo-liibiya-oo-xoojinaya-iskaashiga-socdaalka-iyo-arrimaha-muwaadiniinta/",
