    "content": "Hundreds of teachers in Garowe and surrounding villages in Puntland have lost their jobs following the closure and downsizing of schools due to financial constraints. Among those affected is Jama Mohamud Ahmed, who taught English at Waberi primary and middle school. He earned $200 a month, which supported his nine-member family, covering food, water, rent, and his children's education. Since losing his job, Jama said his family now cooks only once a day and relies on the assistance of relatives, friends, and neighbors.\n\nThe closures and staff reductions stem from ongoing financial difficulties faced by the schools, though specific causes have not been detailed. This situation reflects broader economic challenges in the region, where educational institutions often struggle with limited funding and resources. The layoffs have left many teachers without a steady income, forcing them to seek alternative means of survival.\n\nJama's story is one of many. He explained that his monthly salary was barely enough to meet his family's needs, and its loss has pushed them into a precarious situation. \"We only cook once a day,\" he said. \"We rely on relatives, friends and neighbours when we need help.\" The impact extends beyond the individual teachers to their families and communities, as reduced spending power affects local businesses and the overall social fabric.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments are particularly concerning. Many in the diaspora have family members back home who depend on remittances for basic necessities, including education. When teachers lose their jobs, the quality of education suffers, potentially leading to lower school enrollment and long-term consequences for the region's development. Diaspora communities often support educational initiatives, and these closures may prompt increased calls for assistance or intervention.\n\nThe closure of schools and the resulting unemployment among teachers highlight the fragile state of public services in Puntland. Without immediate solutions, the situation could worsen, leading to a generation of children missing out on education and further economic decline. Local authorities and international organizations may need to step in to address the funding gaps and support affected families.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/09/school-closures-put-hundreds-of-teachers-out-of-work-in-garowe/",
